Make Save, Save As and Exit reliable in the desktop app

Purpose:
Saving must never silently fail, write an empty or stale file, or
destroy the document's history, and quitting must give every document
a chance to save.

Approach:
- Untitled documents: Save As on a never-edited document used to fail,
  because the temp file only appears on the first save_file; it is now
  created up front.
- Save As writes the current content: after save_as the wrapper sends a
  SaveRequested incoming message, which runs localSaveDo. That folds the
  in-progress editing field into the tree before serializing, so the new
  path gets the document as it stands (save_as by itself only copies
  the on-disk file). The renderer waits for that write before Save As
  completes, so "Save" in the close prompt can't destroy the window
  before the content is written.
- Save As onto the document's own path returns early; it used to copy
  the file onto itself and then delete its undo history and swap file.
- Failed save_file/save_as calls are reported to Elm as SaveError, and
  the save indicator shows a red "Save failed" badge (error text as
  tooltip) until the next successful save.
- Exit closes each window, so documents run their close handling (save
  prompt for untitled docs) instead of the app quitting outright.

Constraints:
- The web app's save flow is unchanged; SaveRequested and SaveError are
  desktop-only incoming messages.

